  "note": "fr.wikisource.org, Bible Crampon 1923 (trad. Augustin Crampon, Desclée, 1923) -- ProofreadPage transclusion: the wiki page's ?action=raw is a <pages> transclusion directive with no scripture text at all, so every URL below is ?action=render (expanded HTML), which carries the verse anchors (id=\"{chapter}-{verse}\") and Crampon's own footnotes. One page per book except Psaumes, split by the edition itself into 5 'Livre' parts (1-41, 42-72, 73-89, 90-106, 107-150) behind an index page; both the index and all 5 parts are captured. Also captures the root index, front matter (Avant-propos, Ancien/Nouveau Testament summaries, Livres table of contents, the Wikisource navigation note, and the New Testament dictionary/glossary) and the 16 Illustrations plate pages. robots.txt disallows /w/ for User-agent: *, but action=render is requested via the /wiki/ path, which carries no such disallow -- checked 2026-08-28. Litmus: Luke 1:28 renders 'Je vous salue, pleine de grace ; le Seigneur est avec vous, vous etes benie entre les femmes.' Psalter is numbered Hebrew, not Vulgate (Ps 10 opens 'Pourquoi, Yahweh, te tiens-tu eloigne ?', Vulgate's Ps 9:22; Ps 9 has 21 verses, not the Vulgate's 39), and a footnote at Ps 9/10 in the text itself says so. 'Yahweh' is printed throughout the Old Testament. Deuterocanonical chapter counts follow the Vulgate, not the Hebrew/Greek short forms: Tobit 14, Esther 16, Baruch 6 (incl. the Letter of Jeremiah as ch. 6), Sirach 51, Daniel 14 (incl. Susanna and Bel and the Dragon) -- so only the Psalter's numbering diverges, not which books or chapters are included.",
